---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, UMBRELLA, CROSSROADS } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title={`About ${UMBRELLA.name}`}
  description={`What ${UMBRELLA.name} is, why it has its name, who makes it, and how its pages are written and checked.`}
  section="home"
  searchable={false}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>About {UMBRELLA.name}</h1>
    <p>
      {UMBRELLA.name} is a small site with two parts, built for people who want to see the evidence for themselves, whatever
      they believe. Every claim on it points to a source.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/history/">{SITE.title}</a></strong> traces what happened: the story of Christianity from the first
        century onward, one era at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href={`${CROSSROADS.base}/`}>{CROSSROADS.name}</a></strong> reports what churches officially teach today on
        questions such as baptism, in the words of their own catechisms and confessions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The two are kept apart on purpose. One describes the past and the other describes what churches say now. Neither tells
      you which church is right.
    </p>

    <h2>Why "{UMBRELLA.name}"?</h2>
    <p>
      A cairn is a small stack of stones that marks a path where the way is hard to see. Walkers do not build one for
      themselves. They find it, trust that someone went before them, and often add a stone for the next person.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the idea behind the site. Christians have always had to find their way using what earlier believers left
      behind: writings, creeds, councils and catechisms. {UMBRELLA.name} tries to set those markers out in plain view, each one
      traced to where it came from, so a reader can look at the ground and decide where to walk.
    </p>
    <p>
      The word "way" is there too. The book of Acts says the first Christians were known as followers of "the Way" (Acts
      9:2; 24:14), and the prophets spoke of paths and signposts for God's people. The name of the history section,
      {SITE.title}, comes from one of those passages; see <a href="/history/about/">its About page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who makes it</h2>
    <p>
      {UMBRELLA.name} is made by a Christian, with the aim of presenting the evidence fairly. It is a personal project,
      not the work of a church, a denomination or a publisher.
    </p>

    <h2>How the pages are written and checked</h2>
    <p>
      Pages are drafted with the help of an AI and then checked against their sources. Nothing here has yet been reviewed by a
      specialist, and every page says how far its checking has got. Where the sources do not support a claim, we cut it back
      or leave it out.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/history/about/">How {SITE.title} is checked</a>, including the AI source check and the public review
        records for each era.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${CROSSROADS.base}/about/`}>How {CROSSROADS.name} works</a>: official documents only, one named church body
        per tradition, and no position stated unless a church's own document states it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What {UMBRELLA.name} is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is not a scholarly authority. Please read the works we cite.</li>
      <li>It does not speak for any church. No church has reviewed or endorsed what it says about them.</li>
      <li>It does not rank the churches or say which is correct.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Corrections and reuse</h2>
    <p>
      If something is wrong, misleading or improperly used, please tell us. Each era and each Crossroads entry has a "Report a
      problem" link, and you do not need an account to use it. Reports are read and acted on as time permits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The text we write is free to reuse under the
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a> license. The site's code is
      open source on <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/ancient-paths">GitHub</a>. Quotations, images and maps from
      other sources keep their own licenses, listed on each section's Sources page.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
